\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Schematic principle of the pump-probe experiment: the optical pump pulse excites the sample and the delayed X-ray probe pulse measures its response. Varying the delay between both pulses provides time-resolved snapshots of the relaxation process \cite {0}.}}{2}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Two- and three-dimensional views of the investigated $[\text {Fe}(\text {bipy})_3]^{2+}$ complex. Together, they show both the connectivity of the ligands and the spatial arrangement around the iron centre.}}{3}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Splitting of the five d orbitals of a $d^6$ transition-metal ion in an octahedral ligand field. The field separates the higher-energy $e_g$ orbitals from the lower-energy $t_{2g}$ orbitals and thereby determines whether low-spin or high-spin configurations are favoured \cite {0}.}}{3}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Potential-energy curves describing the excited-state dynamics of $[\text {Fe}(\text {bipy})_3]^{2+}$ in water after excitation into the MLCT band. The diagram demonstrates the suggested relaxation pathways through a transient triplet state and towards the long-lived quintet state \cite {0}.}}{4}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Overview of the FXE optical bench and its diagnostic and focusing components: BIU 1,2,3 (beam imaging units), SpA (spectrum analyser), TAD (time arrival detector), PS 1,2 (power slits), SAA (solid attenuator assembly), IPM1 (intensity position monitor), CRLs (compound refractive lenses) \cite {0}.}}{5}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces X-ray absorption spectrum of $[\text {Fe}(\text {terpy})_2]^{2+}$ at the Fe K edge, showing the XANES and EXAFS regions together with the background contribution $\mu _0$. The spectrum illustrates why the beam energy is chosen above the absorption edge, while avoiding the strongest near-edge structure \cite {0}.}}{7}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Beam images recorded with the same attenuator settings: the pink beam is shown on the left and the monochromatic beam on the right. The comparison makes the strong intensity loss caused by the monochromator visible, despite the improved spectral selectivity.}}{8}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional intensity distributions of the pink beam (left) and the monochromatic beam (right) measured with identical attenuators. The plots show how the monochromator changes the recorded beam intensity while preserving the overall spatial beam profile.}}{8}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Horizontal and vertical intensity profiles of the focused X-ray beam together with Gaussian fits. The resulting FWHM of approximately \qty {13.5}{\mu \m } quantifies the beam size achieved at the focus.}}{9}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces UV/VIS absorption spectrum of an aqueous $[\text {Fe}(\text {bipy})_3]\text {Cl}_2$ solution with the different charge-transfer transitions indicated. The wavelength-dependent absorption is used to estimate suitable concentrations and to compare the available excitation wavelengths \cite {0}.}}{10}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The Von Hamos spectrometer in its operating position, with the Jungfrau detector read-out and the instrument settings shown. The image documents the spectrometer configuration used for the measurements.}}{12}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Spatial intensity profile of the focused X-ray beam at the sample position. Its compact spot defines the region of the liquid jet probed by the X-rays and serves as the reference for checking pump-probe overlap.}}{12}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Spatial intensity profile of the optical excitation laser at the sample position, showing the region excited by the pump pulse. Its size is compared with the X-ray spot to ensure that the probe interrogates laser-excited molecules.}}{13}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Superposition of the optical-laser and X-ray intensity profiles in the sample plane, with the smaller X-ray spot positioned inside the broader laser profile. This overlap is important because it ensures that the probed sample volume is excited while providing tolerance to the optical laser's spatial jitter.}}{13}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Reference X-ray emission spectra for the different spin states together with the corresponding reference difference spectra. These spectra provide the basis for identifying the spin-state contributions in the measured transient signal.}}{14}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces X-ray emission spectrum measured with the excitation laser on at a delay of \qty {100}{ps} (left) and the corresponding laser-on minus laser-off difference spectrum (right). The comparison separates the dominant emission features from the transient changes caused by the optical excitation.}}{14}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Transient X-ray emission difference spectra recorded at different pump-probe delays. The positive feature near \qty {7045}{eV}, the negative minimum near \qty {7058}{eV} and the evolving shoulder around \qty {7054}{eV} change with delay, showing that the emission spectrum responds to the laser excitation and evolves as the spin-state populations change. The relatively small differences between the spectra at \qty {600}{fs} and \qty {800}{fs} suggest that the system is approaching a more stable excited-state distribution.}}{15}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Kinetic traces of the transient signal at four selected emission energies as a function of pump-probe delay. Comparing these energies shows that the spectral features change systematically in time, confirming that the signal contains measurable dynamics rather than isolated statistical fluctuations and providing suitable traces for the subsequent analysis.}}{16}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Spin-state populations obtained by fitting the measured difference spectrum at each pump-probe delay. The singlet dominates before excitation and then decreases, while the triplet population appears transiently before the quintet grows to dominate, consistent with relaxation through a short-lived intermediate towards the high-spin state. The small doublet and quartet contributions are less conclusive and may reflect correlations between reference spectra.}}{17}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Singlet population extracted from the spectral decomposition together with the Gaussian temporal-transition fit. The fitted midpoint $t_0$ marks the effective temporal position of the excitation, while the fit width describes the finite temporal spread of the pump-probe response.}}{17}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional representation of the time-resolved transient X-ray emission spectra. The additional view highlights how the spectral features evolve continuously with the pump-probe delay.}}{D}{figure.caption.26}%
